---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/guides/replenishment-tools";
const title = "Shopify Replenishment Tools \u2014 Evaluate the Reorder Workflow";
const desc = "Choose a replenishment sender through product-family timing, subscription-state tests and incremental margin measurement.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Should every product use the same reminder interval?",
    "answer": "Use product-family evidence and customer context. Gifts, bulk quantities, subscriptions and bundles can require different paths or exclusion."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should disqualify a tool?",
    "answer": "Failure to implement a required eligibility, cancellation or export path without an acceptable owned workaround. Test those paths on the intended connector."
  },
  {
    "question": "How long should the pilot run?",
    "answer": "Long enough to observe the relevant reorder cycle and returns. State the planned window and uncertainty rather than assuming a fixed thirty-day answer."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is attributed revenue enough to justify the reminder?",
    "answer": "No. Compare an appropriate control and consistent contribution measures; otherwise natural reorders can be mistaken for lift."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">← All operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6 max-w-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Decide whether a reminder is the right job</h2>
    <p>This guide is for a store evaluating repeat-purchase automation. Start with a genuinely replenishable product family. A durable product, gift order or collector release may need education or a launch alert instead. Separate a reminder to repurchase from a subscription payment notice or delivery update. Classify message purpose and permission requirements with the store’s responsible reviewer; a previous order alone is not a reason to assume a marketing send is permitted.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Build the timing dataset</h2>
    <p>Collect fulfilled purchase dates, quantities, family identifiers and subsequent orders for the same family. Separate subscriptions, cancellations, refunds, gifts where known and bundles that distort units. Inspect repeat intervals and the observation window: customers who have not yet reordered are not proof that the median repeat buyer represents everyone. A family with sparse repeat history needs a cautious proposed pilot, not a claimed optimal consumption interval. Document data gaps before choosing a timing feature.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Translate consumption into eligibility</h2>
    <p>A proposed tea reminder can use pack size and prior quantity to distinguish a sample from a bulk purchase, while allowing the customer to say they still have stock. A skincare bundle needs a choice of which component is running out rather than a blanket rebuy prompt. These are proposed designs, not observed outcomes. Reset timing after a relevant family purchase; an unrelated accessory order should not automatically reset the consumable clock. Handle substitutions and partial refunds explicitly.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Require a workflow demonstration from each candidate</h2>
    <p>Give Sequenzy or another shortlisted sender the same product-family cases. Ask it to show a fulfilled purchase entering a delayed reminder, a relevant new purchase cancelling it, an unrelated purchase leaving it unchanged, and a refund changing eligibility. Include missing variant IDs, merged profiles and delayed events. Request evidence of how queued messages are rechecked at dispatch. Reject a tool for a required unsupported path, or document an owned workaround with its maintenance cost; do not rank fourteen apps from generic catalog descriptions.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Keep subscriptions and support in the decision</h2>
    <p>Test active, paused, skipped and cancelled subscription states using the actual subscription connector. A skip is not automatically an invitation to send a separate buy-now offer. Route payment recovery to its existing owner. Decide what a late delivery or open product complaint does to the reminder and who resolves it. If the email tool cannot see a necessary state, choose a reliable upstream gate or defer that audience rather than promising native awareness.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Design a proposed pilot around the purchase cycle</h2>
    <p>Choose one family and one timing question. Assign eligible customers to treatment and a persistent no-reminder control where feasible, using the same eligibility and observation window. Keep incentives and channel additions separate from the first timing test. Observe long enough to include the plausible reorder cycle and returns; thirty days is not universally sufficient. Track assignment, sends, family orders, net contribution, opt-outs and support issues. Record uncertainty if the sample is too small to distinguish a useful effect.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Measure profit without double counting natural reorders</h2>
    <p>Compare repeat purchase and contribution per assigned eligible customer, including the control group. Deduct discounts, returns and relevant variable costs consistently. Report total sample sizes and the decision window. Platform attribution can describe interactions but is not the same as a causal lift estimate. A coupon variant may shift order timing or discount an order that would have happened anyway. Inspect later periods before claiming it created durable repeat demand.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Procurement evidence and operational handoff</h2>
    <p>Shopify’s Order reference covers purchase and refund data and notes the access requirement for older orders. The Customer reference includes history and subscription relationships. Confirm that the actual connector supplies the required fields, history and update timing; API availability does not certify an app. Obtain a dated quote for eligible contacts, projected reminders, required modules and exports. Hand over family mappings, timing assumptions, exclusions, event cases, pilot assignment and a pause procedure. Link the detailed offer policy and collision rules rather than duplicating them here.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><p>Source scope: <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Order">Shopify Order reference</a> and <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Customer">Customer reference</a>, checked November 24, 2026. Supplier capabilities and legal conclusions are outside this source scope.</p></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><p>Related: <a class="link" href="/guides/implementation-plan">Shopify Email Implementation Plan</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/migration">Shopify Email Migration</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">Shopify Email Pricing Models</a></p></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
